<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Routines – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 0.9em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Routines</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> First, I Sit Down.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: productive: Name daily activities, using isolated words. interactive: Engage with simple commands using basic words and gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can name daily activities, using isolated words (e.g., 'sit' or 'stand'). Can follow and give very simple commands for interactions (e.g., 'Stand up!' 'I stand up!'). Can identify very simple classroom commands (e.g., 'Stand up' and 'sit down').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with a 'Good morning!' song and leads a "Simon Says" game focused on basic classroom commands. The teacher begins by saying, "Simon says, 'Stand up!'" and performs the action. Then, "Simon says, 'Sit down!'" and performs the action. Students mimic the actions and repeat the commands after the teacher.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models the actions for 'stand up' and 'sit down' using their body. The teacher points to a 'chair' and 'desk' while naming them clearly. The teacher also focuses on the pronunciation of /p/ in 'pick' (e.g., "pick up your pencil") and /d/ in 'down' by exaggerating the sounds and having students repeat chorally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using gestures and simple questions.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher sits) "Am I standing or sitting?" (Students respond: "Sitting!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher stands) "Am I standing up?" (Students respond: "Yes!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to a chair) "Is this a desk or a chair?" (Students respond: "Chair!")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a large, colorful picture sequence chart depicting a simple classroom routine:
                    <ol style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Step 1: Sit down (picture of a child sitting in a chair)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Step 2: Open your bag (picture of a child opening a school bag)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Step 3: Take out your paper (picture of a child taking paper from a bag)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Step 4: Pick up your pen (picture of a child picking up a pen from the desk)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Step 5: Draw a big circle (picture of a child drawing)</li>
                    </ol>
                    The genre is "Instructions / Picture Sequence" for classroom routines.
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding and following basic classroom commands and daily routines expressed with simple language and numerical sequencing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to each picture and models the command: "Step 1: Sit down." Students listen and point to the corresponding picture.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "What is Step 3?" (Students point to the "Take out your paper" picture).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher says a command, "Pick up your pen," and students point to the correct picture.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice naming daily activities using isolated words and responding to simple commands. Grammar: Imperatives (Sit down, Stand up, Pick up, Put on), Sequencing words with numbers (Step 1, Step 2, Step 3). Vocabulary: chair, desk, bag, pen, paper, sit, pick up, put, draw. Pragmatic: Following instructions. Sociolinguistic: Using 'Please'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: "Command Echo & Do" (Choral Repetition with TPR):</b> The teacher calls out a command (e.g., "Sit down!"), performs the action, and students echo the command chorally while performing the action. This is repeated for 'stand up', 'pick up your pen', 'put down your paper', 'open your bag'. The teacher emphasizes pronouncing /p/ and /d/ correctly.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: "Picture Command Guess":</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard depicting an action (e.g., a child picking up a pen). Students, in pairs, whisper the command "Pick up your pen, please!" to each other, then the teacher selects a student to say it aloud. The teacher gives sentence starters: "Please, _______ your paper!" (students complete: "pick up").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction: "Give a Command, Get a Response":</b> Students work in pairs. Student A gives a simple command from the routine chart (e.g., "Stand up, please!"). Student B performs the action and responds with an isolated word or short phrase (e.g., "Stand up!" or "I stand up!"). They then switch roles. The teacher circulates, providing support and correcting pronunciation.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-20 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Fluency focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format: "Routine Leader" Role-Play.</b> Students will work in small groups of 3-4. Each group will have a "Routine Leader" who gives simple commands from the learned routine (e.g., "First, I Sit Down."). The other students in the group will follow the commands and respond with actions and short phrases.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher divides the class into small groups.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group gets a set of flashcards or a mini-chart depicting the routine steps (Sit down, Open bag, Take out paper, Pick up pen, Draw).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student is chosen as the "Routine Leader" for a few minutes. The leader looks at the flashcards and gives commands, e.g., "Step 1: Sit down, please!" The other group members respond by sitting down and saying, "I sit down!"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The leader continues through the routine: "Step 2: Open your bag!" (Others open bag and say, "I open my bag!"). "Step 3: Take out your paper!" (Others take out paper and say, "I take out paper!"). "Step 4: Pick up your pen!" (Others pick up pen and say, "I pick up my pen!").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students rotate the role of "Routine Leader" so everyone gets a chance to give commands. The teacher moves around, observing and offering encouragement, not interrupting fluency for minor errors.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in giving and responding to commands, collaborative interaction within groups, and completing the mini-routine successfully.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment and self-assessment)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during the "Routine Leader" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Routine Leader" role-play in their groups, demonstrating their ability to give and follow commands, and respond with simple words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target verbs (sit, stand, pick up, put) and initial sounds (/p/, /d/).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (chair, desk, bag, pen, paper) when giving or responding to commands.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of commands when acting as "Routine Leader."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to follow commands correctly with actions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of polite expressions (e.g., 'Please').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and engagement with group members.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows two emojis: a happy face and a sad face) "Can you say 'Stand up' and 'Sit down'?" (Students point to the happy face or sad face).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you help your friends?" (Students show thumbs up for 'yes' or thumbs down for 'no').</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Consolidation and Future Steps)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new action words can you say today?" (e.g., sit, stand, pick up, draw)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or a little difficult to give commands to your friends?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., TPR, pair work, the "Routine Leader" role-play encouraged active participation and collaboration). The focus on 'Please' was effective for sociolinguistic competence. Pronunciation practice for /p/ and /d/ was integrated well.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (Some students need more individual prompting to speak. Maybe use larger, clearer flashcards for the routine. Ensure all students get multiple turns as the "Routine Leader").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4, students will continue to use these daily activity words and commands. They will engage in simple writing activities, possibly drawing their own routine steps and labeling them with the words practiced today, building on the vocabulary and understanding of sequences.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>